      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-252738",
      ":question/problem": "Two random variables, $A$ and $B$, are being examined to determine if they are independent. Variable $A$ can take two values $A_{1}$ and $A_{2}$, and variable $B$ can take three values, $B_{1}$, $B_{2}$, and $B_{3}$.\n\nThe following contingency table reports the frequency distribution of the two features for a sample of $100$ individuals.\n\nFill in the missing values of the expected frequencies under the hypothesis that the variables are independent. Round your final answers to one decimal place, where appropriate.\n\n|  | $B_{1}$ | $B_{2}$ | $B_{3}$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $A_{1}$ | $O_{11} = 10$<br>$E_{11} =$ {{field-1}} | $O_{12} = 13$<br>$E_{12} =$ {{field-2}} | $O_{13} = 16$<br>$E_{13} =$ {{field-3}} |\n| $A_{2}$ | $O_{21} = 23$<br>$E_{21} =$ {{field-4}} | $O_{22} = 28$<br>$E_{22} =$ {{field-5}} | $O_{23} = 10$<br>$E_{23} =$ {{field-6}} |\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."
    },
